{"name":"Cope / copium","deep":true,"image":null,"mentions":1006,"casual":{"lead":["'Cope' is forum slang for something people tell themselves to feel better. In looksmaxxing it is used to sort methods into those considered real and those considered wishful; in blackpill spaces it is also aimed at self-improvement and personality itself.","Calling something cope marks it as self-deceptive and tends to end discussion of whether it might help. On tier lists, low-rated methods such as thumbpulling are labelled this way. It is slang for argument, not a technique, and researchers have not studied the term directly."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People use it in forum replies and tier lists, aimed at others' claims or at their own reassurances, and it influences which methods get attention."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Communities that use it heavily are linked in studies with fatalistic beliefs and mental-health burdens. The word itself has no documented physical risk."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[],"links":{"studies":[{"title":"The Aphasia Brief COPE.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41124307/","source":"American journal of speech-language pathology","year":2025,"cites":null,"kind":null},{"title":"A brief intervention decreases student distress.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37632285/","source":"The clinical teacher","year":2024,"cites":null,"kind":"Randomized Controlled Trial"},{"title":"GABAergic Involvement in Selective Attention.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36976900/","source":"Journal of cognitive neuroscience","year":2023,"cites":null,"kind":"Randomized Controlled Trial"},{"title":"Social-networks use as adaptive or maladaptive strategy for coping with stress.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37482602/","source":"Scientific reports","year":2023,"cites":null,"kind":"Randomized Controlled Trial"},{"title":"Allostatic Load and Its Impact on Health: A Systematic Review.","url":"https://pubmed.nc